course: dictionary
lesson: 4508
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. Ask yourself what the sentence is about. «The hazel dormouse can hibernate for six months or more, after fattening up on hazelnuts and berries in autumn.» Hazelnuts and autumn tell you that hazel is a tree or bush here.
explain: Hazel can also describe a colour. «Hazel eyes» are greenish-brown or light brown, a mix of green and brown. The clue is the word after it: eyes, not a tree or an animal.
explain: Hazel can be a name too. A capital letter in the middle of a sentence is a strong clue that a name is meant, because names start with capital letters.
fact: Hazel can be a noun (a small tree or bush that grows hazelnuts), an adjective (a greenish-brown colour), or a name.
fact: Hazel bushes grow in hedges and woods, and their catkins hang like lambs' tails in late winter.
fact: «The hazel dormouse is a small, gold-brown rodent with a fluffy tail.» It lives in woods and hedges in Britain and Europe, and it is now rare in Britain.
fact: «Hazel eyes» are a mix of green and brown, so the colour is between the two.
fact: Hazel is also a girl's name, so it starts with a capital letter.
exercise: «Her hazel eyes sparkled in the sun.» What does hazel describe here? | A hot, sunny day | A tall tree in the garden | *The colour of her eyes | Her name, said loudly
exercise: «The hazel dormouse gets very fat in autumn on hazelnuts and berries.» Which word is the clue that hazel means a tree or bush? | autumn | *hazelnuts | gets | sleeps
exercise: «Hazel is the name of my cousin, who lives in Leeds.» What is Hazel in this sentence? | A tree | *A girl's name | A small golden animal | A colour
test: «Hazel bushes grow thick in the hedges, and their nuts feed squirrels.» What is hazel in this sentence? | *A tree or bush | A bird | A colour | A name
test: «Mia has hazel eyes, and her brother has blue ones.» What does hazel mean here? | *A greenish-brown colour | A kind of nut | A girl's name | A rare animal in Britain
exam: «Hazel ran to the shop to buy hazelnuts for the squirrels.» What is Hazel in this sentence? | A small rodent with a fluffy tail | A tree or bush | A greenish-brown colour | *A girl's name
